  "summary": "Recent preprints dissect the genomic basis of rapid adaptation, from evolutionary rescue in beetles and flies to selection on archaic human variation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the genomic architecture of rapid adaptation under strong selection, with evolutionary rescue as the central framing in two items. In cowpea seed beetles, rapid and repeatable adaptation to lentil depends on large non-recombining haploblocks on chromosomes 1 and 9. In an outdoor Drosophila insecticide experiment, half of populations went extinct despite strong parallel polygenic adaptation, and pre-adaptation is reported in relation to these outcomes, placing contingency and determinism at the center of rescue. Related work examines selection across time: pooled sequencing of 12 replicate Drosophila populations from long-term experimental evolution maps genomic regions under selection for chronotype divergence, while a Drosophila suzukii study tests whether seasonal adaptive tracking underlies invasion success. A human-genetics preprint reports 25 high-confidence adaptive archaic introgression loci in Peruvians, with temporally dynamic selection on Neanderthal- or Denisovan-like variants. What is new is the explicit linkage of haplotype structure, temporal dynamics, and population persistence.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
